        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy a Zofdim Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A Zofdim pad could be a reasonable buy</strong> if you find a listing that passes the
            checks above, the price is clearly below the checked alternatives, you weigh comfortably less
            than its lowest stated limit, and you will use it for walking. In that case the main risk is
            that it wears out sooner than a better-documented pad, and a good return window and a modest
            price limit that risk.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy one of the checked pads instead</strong> if the listing contradicts itself, has no
            model number or warranty length, comes from a seller you cannot identify, or costs about the
            same as a pad from a known brand. Buy a better-built machine if you are heavy, tall, want to
            jog, or will walk for hours a day; our <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a> explains what to pay for.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you find something we could not — a Zofdim website, a manual, or a listing with a model
            number — the checklist above still applies, and it is worth comparing that model number with
            the pads on this page before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Marketplace Walking Pad</h2>
          <p>These are the errors we see most often with cheap pads sold under unfamiliar names, and each has a simple fix.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the horsepower badge</h3>
              <p>A 3.5 HP claim on a budget pad is a peak figure and tells you little about how hard the motor works for an hour. Compare capacity, belt size and warranty instead, and favour listings that give a continuous rating.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying at the stated weight limit</h3>
              <p>Listings and manuals often disagree on capacity. Assume the lower figure and leave at least 50 lb of margin. A pad run at its limit every day wears its belt, deck and motor quickly.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not reading who pays for returns</h3>
              <p>A 50 lb pad costs real money to ship back. Before you order, read the listing’s own return terms, not just the marketplace’s general policy, and keep the box until you are sure.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Treating the brand name as a guarantee</h3>
              <p>Marketplace labels can change products, or the same product can carry several labels. Check the model number, photos and dimensions against other listings and against the CPSC database.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
